Backstroke, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "30 July –  1 August 1952"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/375"} "Helsingin Uimastadion, Helsinki"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/5282"} "38 from 25 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Earlier in 1952 the world record had been traded between the Frenchman " [:a {:href "/athletes/46810"} "Gilbert Bozon"] "  and the man who had won the title in London, America’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/51573"} "Allan Stack"] ". By the time the Helsinki Games came around the man in the best form turned out to be another American, the Japanese-Hawaiian " [:a {:href "/athletes/51516"} "Yoshi Oyakawa"] ". Oyakawa recorded the fastest times at all stages of the competition and broke the Olympic record, which had stood since 1936, in both semi-final and final. A minor piece of history occurred in heat 5 of the first round when the Soviet Union’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/50887"} "Leonid Sagayduk"] "  raced against the sole Chinese competitor in Helsinki, " [:a {:href "/athletes/46131"} "Wu Chuanyu"] ". The rest of the Chinese team had been stranded in Leningrad due to a visa problem and were unable to reach Helsinki. It was not until thirty-six years later in Seoul that competitors from these two countries would meet again at a Summer Olympics."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "598"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/51516"} "Yoshi Oyakawa"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "1:06.0 (1 h